    "result_text": "The build succeeds and the test passes. The suite is green: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` and `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `sharesHousehold` / `householdId` feature on `POST /api/owners`, following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions.\n\nThe existing code already had `sharesHousehold` as a write-only input and a `RejectDuplicateHousehold` step that *allowed* a same-lastName + same-address owner when the flag was set, but it never assigned or returned a `householdId`. I added that missing half:\n\n**New behavior** \u2014 `AssignHousehold` (`rest/function/owner/AssignHousehold.java`), a transactional step wired into `owners.POST.yml` between `assignCustomerCode` and `save`:\n- When `sharesHousehold` is true and an existing owner shares the household (same last name + address, normalized case-insensitively with collapsed whitespace \u2014 the same identity key `RejectDuplicateHousehold` uses), it derives a **stable, deterministic** `householdId` (a name-based UUID over the normalized last name + address) and assigns it to the new owner **and** every existing household member (backfilling and saving them within the same transaction, so all members always agree). If the flag is unset or no one shares the household, no id is assigned (stays null).\n\n**Supporting changes to surface/persist the field:**\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added a read-only `householdId` string to the `Owner` schema (so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct auto-maps it onto GET responses).\n- `Owner.java` \u2014 new `household_id` column + getter/setter.\n- `OwnerMapper` \u2014 ignore `householdId` when mapping from `OwnerFieldsDto` (consistent with `customerCode`).\n- `db/{hsqldb,h2,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql` \u2014 added the `household_id` column.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreJoinersShareHouseholdId` (owners B and C joining A's household both read back an equal, non-blank `householdId` via `GET /api/owners/{id}`) passes.\n\nOne thing worth flagging: `PUT /api/owners/{ownerId}` does **not** run the household chain, so editing an owner's name/address later won't recompute or clear `householdId` \u2014 the spec only described the create path, so I scoped it there.",
